// ============================================================================
// ecs.z  --  an entity-component-system and the game loop around it.
// ============================================================================
//
// The second program written against rules.z. json.z was a JSON parser: a
// tree of sums, built once, walked recursively, freed all at once. It found
// the sum projection, `try`, `match`-as-expression and `self`-in-an-instance.
//
// It could not reach five things, and this file was chosen to hit all five:
//
//     1. ARITHMETIC ON USER TYPES.        json.z added no two values of its
//                                         own types. `Vec2` does, and with
//                                         `+` an instruction on primitives
//                                         it has to spell every one out.
//     2. HETEROGENEOUS STORAGE.           json.z erased exactly one value
//                                         (`ptr<Writer>`) and stored none. An
//                                         ECS is a bag of `ptr<Shape>`.
//     3. CALLABLES STORED IN DATA.        json.z passed one closure to one
//                                         function. An event bus keeps them.
//     4. MUTATION IN PLACE.               json.z only ever built and printed.
//                                         A simulation writes back every tick.
//     5. TEARDOWN OF THE UNKNOWN.         json.z used an arena and never
//                                         freed a node. A world despawns.
//
// Same convention as json.z: everything is rules.z's syntax as-is except
// where marked [P#], which points at a numbered proposal in FINDINGS. [W#]
// marks something that held up and must not be lost. Numbering continues from
// json.z -- wins start at [W8], frictions at 13, proposals at [P17].
//
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// imports
// ============================================================================
//
// `List` and `Map` graduated from json.z into std between the two files; Map
// is unchanged from json.z's open-addressed version.

std   :: import("std")
mem   :: std.mem
io    :: std.io
fmt   :: std.fmt
core  :: std.core

Allocator :: mem.Allocator
Writer    :: io.Writer
List      :: std.list.List
Map       :: std.map.Map
Key       :: std.key.Key
Option    :: core.Option
Result    :: core.Result
Fn        :: core.Fn
Fn2       :: core.Fn2


// ============================================================================
// game.vec2  --  a user type spelling its own arithmetic
// ============================================================================
//
// `+ - *` are instructions on f32 and they stop there. A user type publishes
// MEMBERS instead, and the call site names the type whose arithmetic it is
// asking for. Nothing is attached from outside and nothing needs to be: the
// members sit in the block that already declares the fields, which is [W2]
// from json.z doing one more job.
//
// This is the section the file was written to exercise and it is the section
// the language lost. The argument the other way was [P17], and it was a good
// one -- `2.0 * v` with nobody owning f32, two additions for one type, a
// `lerp` generic over anything numeric. It lost anyway, to the one thing it
// could not answer: a policy is reached BY NAME, every instance is now named,
// and `+` is the one call site that has no name to reach with. [P17] is
// recorded below as the argument it was.

Vec2 :: (x: f32, y: f32) -> self {
    add   :: (self, b: Vec2) -> Vec2 { Vec2(self.x + b.x, self.y + b.y) }
    sub   :: (self, b: Vec2) -> Vec2 { Vec2(self.x - b.x, self.y - b.y) }
    scale :: (self, s: f32)  -> Vec2 { Vec2(self.x * s,   self.y * s)   }
    eq    :: (self, b: Vec2) -> bool { self.x == b.x && self.y == b.y   }
}

// The second addition. It was a second `Add<Vec2, Vec2>` and reachable only
// by name, because a member cannot be written twice -- which was the whole of
// why `+` was a policy. With `add` a member there is no first instance for it
// to be second to, and the call site does not notice: `saturating.add(a, b)`
// is what it always was, and that is all the naming ever bought.
saturating :: -> self {
    add :: (a: Vec2, b: Vec2) -> Vec2 { Vec2(clamp_unit(a.x + b.x), clamp_unit(a.y + b.y)) }
}

clamp_unit :: (v: f32) -> f32 { if (v > 1.0) { 1.0 } else { if (v < -1.0) { -1.0 } else { v } } }

length_sq :: (v: Vec2) -> f32 { v.x * v.x + v.y * v.y }

// `lerp :: <T> (a: T, b: T, t: f32) -> T { a + (b - a) * t }` -- a generic
// over anything numeric, which read as arithmetic and was sayable only while
// arithmetic was a policy. It is the one thing in this file with no
// replacement at all: no instruction is generic over `T`, so `lerp` is a
// function per type and its body reads as a chain rather than as a formula.
// That is what [P17] was buying, priced.
lerp :: (a: Vec2, b: Vec2, t: f32) -> Vec2 { a.add(b.sub(a).scale(t)) }


// ============================================================================
// ecs.entity
// ============================================================================
//
// A generational handle: the id indexes the storage, the generation says the
// slot has not been recycled underneath you. It was one line, as `Span` and
// `Error` were in json.z, until equality had to be a member and gave it a
// body.

Entity :: (id: u32, gen: u32) -> self {
    eq :: (self, b: Entity) -> bool { self.id == b.id && self.gen == b.gen }
}

// A member and a policy, and the difference between them is the point.
// Equality is a member because there is one of it: two handles are the same
// handle or they are not. `Key` is a policy because a Map is allowed to
// compare its keys differently from `eq`, and it carries hash AND equals in
// one block because those two must agree -- json.z's [P11]. This one does not
// differ, so `equals` delegates to the member rather than restating it.
//
// A name is now the only way to reach an instance, so both `Map`s below say
// `KY = entity_key`. Two declarations, three words each: that is the whole of
// what the pool was saving here, and the whole of what it was hiding.
entity_key :: -> Key<Entity> {
    hash   = (k) -> u64  { (k.id * 2654435761) ^ (k.gen * 40503) }
    equals = (a, b) -> bool { a.eq(b) }
}

NO_ENTITY :: Entity(0, 0)


// ============================================================================
// ecs.store  --  a sparse set. Dense array for iteration, map for lookup.
// ============================================================================
//
// This is the container that makes an ECS fast, and writing it found the
// sharpest result in the file: FRICTION 17, a container that still cannot be
// fully encapsulated, because iterating one means reaching into its fields.

Store :: <C, M: Allocator> (allocator: M) -> self {

    dense := List<C, M>(allocator)          // the components themselves
    owner := List<Entity, M>(allocator)     // dense[i] belongs to owner[i]
    index := Map<Entity, u32, M, KY = entity_key>(allocator)  // entity -> dense index

    count :: (&self) -> u32 { self.dense.count }

    has :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool {
        match (self.index.get(e)) { some (_) { true }  none { false } }
    }

    // Returns a POINTER, not a value, and that is not a style choice.
    // `get` returning `Option<C>` gives you a copy, and a movement system that
    // writes the new position back has to hash the entity a second time to
    // store it. So the reader of a mutable container hands back an address.
    //
    // Not `-> &C`: a fallible lookup is not a place, because there is no such
    // thing as maybe-a-location. `self.dense[i]` IS a place, so `&` takes its
    // address and the match arm at the call site derefs it.
    at :: (&self, e: Entity) -> Option<ptr<C>> {
        match (self.index.get(e)) {
            none     { Option<ptr<C>>.none() }
            some (i) { Option<ptr<C>>.some(&self.dense[i]) }
        }
    }

    add :: (&self, e: Entity, c: C) -> () {
        match (self.index.get(e)) {
            some (i) { self.dense[i] = c }            // overwrite in place
            none     {
                self.index.put(e, self.dense.count)
                self.dense.push(c)
                self.owner.push(e)
            }
        }
    }

    // Swap-remove, so the dense array stays dense. The moved element's index
    // has to be repaired, which is the one place a sparse set is subtle.
    remove :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool {
        i := match (self.index.get(e)) { some (i) { i }  none { return false } }
        last := self.dense.count - 1
        if (i != last) {
            self.dense[i] = self.dense[last]
            moved := self.owner[last]
            self.owner[i] = moved
            self.index.put(moved, i)
        }
        // Three results that go nowhere -- an `Option<C>` and an
        // `Option<Entity>` from `pop`, and a bool from `Map.remove`. A value
        // may not be dropped silently, so each one has to say so out loud.
        _ := self.dense.pop()
        _ := self.owner.pop()
        _ := self.index.remove(e)
        true
    }

    // Iteration is over the dense arrays, by index, because there is no
    // `for (x in xs)` form and an iterator block per store per system cost
    // more syntax than the index loop it replaced -- which is why there is no
    // longer an iterator shape to build one from. See FRICTION 18.
    entity_at :: (&self, i: u32) -> Entity  { self.owner[i] }
    slot_at   :: (&self, i: u32) -> ptr<C>  { &self.dense[i] }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () {
        self.index.deinit()
        self.owner.deinit()
        self.dense.deinit()
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.store  --  erasing it
// ============================================================================
//
// [W8] A GENERIC TYPE ERASES TO A NON-GENERIC SHAPE, as long as no hole in the
// shape mentions the type parameter. `Store<Vec2, M>` and `Store<Health, M>`
// are different types with different vtables, and both coerce to
// `ptr<AnyStore>` because `remove`, `count` and `deinit` never say `C`.
//
// That is NOTE 3's rule ("multiply the vtables, do not add defaults") read
// backwards, and it is what makes a heterogeneous component registry possible
// at all. It is the single largest thing that worked in this file.

AnyStore :: -> self {
    remove: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool
    count:  (&self) -> u32
    deinit: (&self) -> ()
}

// STRUCTURAL MATCHING HAS NO ADAPTER, and this file is where that costs
// something json.z never paid: `Store` satisfies `AnyStore` only because I
// NAMED its members `remove`, `count` and `deinit` to match. Had `Store` come
// from another library and called them `erase`, `len` and `dispose`, there
// would be no way to say "it satisfies AnyStore via this renaming". You write
// a wrapper block per mismatch, and a wrapper costs a pointer, a vtable and a
// name.
//
//     StoreAsAny :: <C, M> (inner: ptr<Store<C, M>>) -> self {
//         remove :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool { self.inner.erase(e) }
//         count  :: (&self) -> u32 { self.inner.len() }
//         deinit :: (&self) -> ()  { self.inner.dispose() }
//     }
//
// Go has the same hole and lives with it. Worth writing down rather than
// discovering it in a user's bug report.


// ============================================================================
// game.components
// ============================================================================

Health :: (hp: i32, max: i32) -> self

// A state machine as a sum, with the state's data in the state. This is what
// `-> oneof` is for and it reads exactly right.
Ai :: -> oneof {
    idle:  ()
    hunt:  Entity            // who we are chasing
    flee:  Vec2              // where we are running to
    dead:  ()
}

// FRICTION 20, which json.z raised as FRICTION 7 and which is much worse here.
// "Is this entity dead" is a tag test with no payload to bind, and there is no
// way to say it. Every such question becomes a match with a tail of dead arms:
is_dead :: (a: Ai) -> bool {
    match (a) { dead { true }  idle { false }  hunt (_) { false }  flee (_) { false } }
}
// The ECS version of the same gap is worse than a bool: a real ECS keys its
// archetypes off a COMPONENT BITMASK, and a bitmask needs each variant to have
// an ordinal. A sum's discriminant is not a value, cannot be compared, cannot
// index an array, and cannot be serialised. See [P19].


// ============================================================================
// ecs.world
// ============================================================================
//
// FRICTION 21, the largest new result in the file: A `-> self` BLOCK HAS NO
// CONSTRUCTOR BODY, so a self-referential structure cannot be built.
//
// What the World wants is four typed stores AND a list of pointers to those
// same stores, so that despawn and teardown can sweep every component type
// without naming it. Written the obvious way:
//
//     World :: <M: Allocator> (allocator: M) -> self {
//         pos    := Store<Vec2, M>(allocator)
//         hp     := Store<Health, M>(allocator)
//         stores := List<ptr<AnyStore>, M>(allocator)
//         stores.push(&self.pos)          // <- no `self` here, and no
//         stores.push(&self.hp)           //    statement position either
//     }
//
// ONE thing stops it, and it is about syntax: there is no statement position
// in a `-> self` block. A field initialiser is an expression; there is
// nowhere to run a step that is not a binding. json.z hit this as FRICTION 5
// and read it as being about FAILURE, and [P14] (`-> Result<self, E>`) was
// adopted for it. [P14] is the wrong half of the fix: it gives the
// constructor a RETURN TYPE and still no BODY. `try` in a field initialiser
// has nothing to unwind to.
//
// What does NOT stop it is `&self.pos`. A value has one address for as long
// as it is alive, so a pointer into a block survives that block being handed
// to the caller, and a structure pointing into itself means what it says.
// That half is answered; the body to write it in is what is missing.
//
// The workaround below is two-phase construction, which is exactly what the
// unified declaration form was supposed to abolish: a `wire` you must remember
// to call, and a World that is unsafe to use between the two lines.

World :: <M: Allocator> (allocator: M) -> self {

    next_id := 1
    recycle := List<Entity, M>(allocator)       // despawned ids, ready for reuse
    live    := Map<Entity, bool, M, KY = entity_key>(allocator)

    pos   := Store<Vec2, M>(allocator)
    vel   := Store<Vec2, M>(allocator)
    hp    := Store<Health, M>(allocator)
    brain := Store<Ai, M>(allocator)

    // Declared here, defined two hundred lines below. Order independence is
    // load-bearing again, and this is the second program that needs it.
    died  := Signal<Entity, M>(allocator)

    // Bound here and POPULATED by `wire`, below, because populating it needs
    // `self` and there is nowhere in this block that `self` exists.
    stores := List<ptr<AnyStore>, M>(allocator)
    wired  := false

    // PHASE TWO. Everything that needs `self` lives here, and the type is
    // unusable until it has run.
    wire :: (&self) -> () {
        if (self.wired) { return }
        self.stores.push(&self.pos)
        self.stores.push(&self.vel)
        self.stores.push(&self.hp)
        self.stores.push(&self.brain)
        self.wired = true
    }

    spawn :: (&self) -> Entity {
        e := match (self.recycle.pop()) {
            some (old) { Entity(old.id, old.gen + 1) }
            none       { id := self.next_id; self.next_id += 1; Entity(id, 1) }
        }
        self.live.put(e, true)
        e
    }

    // The erased sweep, and the reason `stores` exists: despawn must remove
    // this entity from EVERY component type, and it must not know which types
    // those are. Three of the five items in this file's header meet here.
    despawn :: (&self, e: Entity) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < self.stores.count; i += 1) {
            self.stores[i].remove(e)             // indirect call, one per type
        }
        self.live.remove(e)
        self.recycle.push(e)
    }

    alive :: (&self, e: Entity) -> bool {
        match (self.live.get(e)) { some (b) { b }  none { false } }
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () {
        // By hand, in reverse declaration order, because there is no
        // automatic destruction and `deinit` is a naming convention.
        // NOT through `stores` either: that would be four indirect calls to
        // reach four fields that are statically right here. Erasure earns its
        // keep for `despawn`, which is per-entity and dynamic, and not for
        // teardown, which is per-type and static.
        self.brain.deinit()
        self.hp.deinit()
        self.vel.deinit()
        self.pos.deinit()
        self.stores.deinit()
        self.died.deinit()
        self.live.deinit()
        self.recycle.deinit()
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.query  --  joining two stores
// ============================================================================
//
// FRICTION 23. The natural signature for a join is
//
//     next :: (&self) -> Option<{e: Entity, a: ptr<A>, b: ptr<B>}>
//
// -- json.z's [W4], the partial projection, used as a RESULT TYPE. It does not
// work, and the reason is worth stating: `-> {a, b}` names no type. It is a
// projection, which is a property of a DECLARATION, and `Option<T>` needs a
// TYPE. So the multiple-return form, which json.z found to be the thing you
// reach for constantly, does not compose with generics: the moment a
// multiple-return has to be stored, wrapped in an Option, or put in a list, it
// must be given a name and become an ordinary record.
//
// Naming it brings the plumbing parameters back with it, which is json.z's
// FRICTION 1 arriving at a second, unrelated site.

Hit :: <A, B> (e: Entity, a: ptr<A>, b: ptr<B>) -> self

Join2 :: <A, B, M> (xs: ptr<Store<A, M>>, ys: ptr<Store<B, M>>) -> self {

    i := 0

    // Iterate the smaller store and probe the larger -- the standard join, and
    // the reason `count` is on the store at all.
    next :: (&self) -> Option<Hit<A, B>> {
        while (self.i < self.xs.count()) {
            e := self.xs.entity_at(self.i)
            a := self.xs.slot_at(self.i)
            self.i += 1
            match (self.ys.at(e)) {
                some (b) { return Option<Hit<A, B>>.some(Hit(e, a, b)) }
                none     { }
            }
        }
        Option<Hit<A, B>>.none()
    }
}

// FRICTION 24. `Join2`, `Join3`, `Join4`. json.z's FRICTION 6 called the
// `Fn`/`Fn2` family "cheap enough to live with". At a query API it is not
// cosmetic any more: a system that reads three components and writes a fourth
// is the ordinary case, and the arity family is now part of the surface a user
// of the library has to learn.

join2 :: <A, B, M> (xs: ptr<Store<A, M>>, ys: ptr<Store<B, M>>) -> Join2<A, B, M> {
    Join2(xs, ys)
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.system
// ============================================================================
//
// A shape, so systems can be held in one list and run in order. `M` is here
// only because `World` has it, which is json.z's FRICTION 2 (monomorphisation
// is viral through containing types) reappearing in a SHAPE rather than in
// data. The alias trick that fixed it for `Json` does not apply: a library
// cannot name the application's allocator, so `System` stays generic and every
// implementer of it does too.

System :: <M: Allocator> -> self {
    run:  (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> ()
    name: (&self) -> slice<u8>
}

// A stateless system is a block with no fields, so it is zero-sized, and the
// `ptr<System<M>>` in the schedule is one word plus a shared vtable.
Movement :: <M: Allocator> () -> self {
    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "movement" }
    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        it := join2(&w.pos, &w.vel)
        loop {
            h := match (it.next()) { some (h) { h }  none { return } }
            // Mutation in place, through the pointer the join yielded.
            // `h.a.* = ...` -- the pointee is written, and nothing in the type
            // says `run` may do that while `name` may not. FRICTION 25.
            //
            // It used to read `h.a.* + h.b.* * dt`. An integration step is the
            // one expression a reader takes in by its shape, and the shape is
            // what the member call costs -- see [P17].
            h.a.* = h.a.*.add(h.b.*.scale(dt))
        }
    }
}

// A system WITH state: the tuning constant is a field, so it is a runtime
// param of the block, which is json.z's [W2] again -- params of a `-> self`
// def are the fields, and a configured system needs no new form.
Decay :: <M: Allocator> (rate: f32) -> self {
    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "decay" }
    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < w.vel.count(); i += 1) {
            v := w.vel.slot_at(i)
            v.* = v.*.scale(1.0 - self.rate * dt)
        }
    }
}

Reaper :: <M: Allocator> (doomed: ptr<List<Entity, M>>) -> self {
    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "reaper" }
    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        // Collect first, despawn second: `despawn` swap-removes inside every
        // store, so mutating while iterating a dense array is the same bug it
        // is in every other language. Nothing in the type system helps.
        self.doomed.count = 0
        for (i := 0; i < w.hp.count(); i += 1) {
            h := w.hp.slot_at(i)
            if (h.hp <= 0) { self.doomed.push(w.hp.entity_at(i)) }
        }
        for (i := 0; i < self.doomed.count; i += 1) {
            e := self.doomed[i]
            w.died.emit(e)                     // see Signal, below
            w.despawn(e)
        }
    }
}

// The schedule. Every element is a fat pointer and ERASURE IS A BORROW, so
// nothing here owns the pointees: the concrete `Movement`, `Decay` and
// `Reaper` blocks have to be locals in main that outlive the schedule. There
// is no way to store a shape BY VALUE and no box to put one in.
Schedule :: <M: Allocator> (allocator: M) -> self {
    items := List<ptr<System<M>>, M>(allocator)

    add :: (&self, s: ptr<System<M>>) -> () { self.items.push(s) }

    tick :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < self.items.count; i += 1) { self.items[i].run(w, dt) }
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () { self.items.deinit() }
}


// ============================================================================
// ecs.signal  --  callables stored in data
// ============================================================================
//
// json.z passed one closure to one function and it worked ([P15]: a closure is
// a block with a `()` member). KEEPING one is a different question, and it is
// the third thing this file was written to find out.

Signal :: <E, M: Allocator> (allocator: M) -> self {

    subs := List<ptr<Fn<E, ()>>, M>(allocator)

    // The same borrow, concretely. The argument is a fat pointer, so the
    // CALLER owns the closure block and must keep it alive for as long as the
    // signal lives. In main below, the subscriber is a local declared before
    // the world and never mentioned again -- if it went out of scope first,
    // this list would hold two dangling words and nothing would say so.
    on :: (&self, f: ptr<Fn<E, ()>>) -> () { self.subs.push(f) }

    // FRICTION 27. There is no `off`. Unsubscribing means finding the
    // subscriber again, and a `ptr<Fn<E,()>>` is a PAIR of words built at the
    // coercion site. Two coercions of the same block are two fat pointers; are
    // they equal? rules.z says a fat pointer is "built at the moment of
    // coercion, and only there", which suggests yes for the data half and
    // nothing at all about the vtable half. Identity of an erased value is
    // undefined, so an observer list can be added to and never pruned.
    emit :: (&self, e: E) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < self.subs.count; i += 1) {
            self.subs[i](e)                   // -> self.subs[i].()(e)  [P15]
        }
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () { self.subs.deinit() }
}

// A subscriber that has to mutate the thing that is emitting to it. This is
// the ordinary case for an event bus and it is where FRICTION 25 stops being
// theoretical: `w` is a `ptr<World<M>>` captured by a closure that `World`
// itself calls, from inside `Reaper.run`, while `Reaper.run` is iterating
// `w.hp`. Nothing in any of the three signatures says who may write what, so
// nothing can flag the reentrancy.
on_death :: <M: Allocator> (log: ptr<List<Entity, M>>) -> self {
    () :: (&self, e: Entity) -> () { self.log.push(e) }
}


// ============================================================================
// game.combat  --  a system that reads a sum and writes two components
// ============================================================================

Combat :: <M: Allocator> (bite: i32) -> self {

    name :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { "combat" }

    run :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, dt: f32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < w.brain.count(); i += 1) {
            e := w.brain.entity_at(i)
            a := w.brain.slot_at(i)

            // A state machine step. The match is an EXPRESSION, so the whole
            // transition is one assignment -- json.z's best structural result,
            // and it holds here.
            a.* = match (a.*) {
                idle       { self.wake(w, e) }
                hunt (t)   { self.chase(w, e, t) }
                flee (to)  { self.run_away(w, e, to) }
                dead       { Ai.dead() }
            }
        }
    }

    wake :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity) -> Ai {
        match (self.nearest(w, e)) {
            some (t) { Ai.hunt(t) }
            none     { Ai.idle() }
        }
    }

    chase :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity, t: Entity) -> Ai {
        if (!w.alive(t)) { return Ai.idle() }

        // Four pointers out of two stores, each an Option, each needing an
        // arm. `match ... { some (x) { x }  _ { return } }` per line is
        // json.z's binding idiom and it is still the right shape -- but see
        // FRICTION 28: every one of these is a hash lookup that the join
        // above already did, and there is no way to hold onto the result.
        me   := match (w.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Ai.idle() } }
        prey := match (w.pos.at(t)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Ai.idle() } }

        d := prey.*.sub(me.*)
        if (length_sq(d) < 1.0) {
            match (w.hp.at(t)) {
                some (h) { h.hp -= self.bite }
                none     { }
            }
            return Ai.hunt(t)
        }

        match (w.vel.at(e)) {
            some (v) { v.* = d.scale(0.5) }
            none     { }
        }
        Ai.hunt(t)
    }

    run_away :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity, to: Vec2) -> Ai {
        me := match (w.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Ai.idle() } }
        if (length_sq(to.sub(me.*)) < 1.0) { return Ai.idle() }
        Ai.flee(to)
    }

    nearest :: (&self, w: ptr<World<M>>, e: Entity) -> Option<Entity> {
        me   := match (w.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { p }  none { return Option<Entity>.none() } }
        best := Option<Entity>.none()
        bd   := 1000000.0
        for (i := 0; i < w.hp.count(); i += 1) {
            t := w.hp.entity_at(i)
            if (t.eq(e)) { continue }
            match (w.pos.at(t)) {
                some (p) {
                    d := length_sq(p.*.sub(me.*))
                    if (d < bd) { bd = d; best = Option<Entity>.some(t) }
                }
                none { }
            }
        }
        best
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// game.print
// ============================================================================

vec2_fmt :: -> fmt.Formattable<Vec2> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        io.write(w, "(")
        fmt.fmt_f32.format(v.x, w)
        io.write(w, ", ")
        fmt.fmt_f32.format(v.y, w)
        io.write(w, ")")
    }
}

// A PARAMETERISED policy instance. Every instance is named now, so that half
// has stopped being remarkable; taking compile-time arguments has not, and
// nothing in json.z actually exercised one. Three things show up at once:
//
//   - `indent: u32 = 2` is a COMPILE-TIME VALUE parameter, the third kind of
//     compile-time argument, and exactly what `capacity :: 10` in `List` is:
//     an ordinary compile-time binding that happens to sit in a parameter
//     list rather than in a block.
//
//   - `M` must be inferred and `indent` must be given, so the caller cannot
//     pass positionally and must write `dump<indent = 4>`. Named compile-time
//     arguments stop being a convenience and become the only way to call this.
//
//   - `self.row(...)` is [P16] carrying `indent` down with it. Written
//     `dump.row(...)` the nested levels would silently reset to 2. Two
//     spellings, two meanings, and that difference is the whole reason `self`
//     exists inside an instance. There used to be a third spelling here --
//     `Formattable<World<M>>.row(...)`, which did not compile because `row` is
//     a fresh member and not one of the type's holes -- and it is not a
//     spelling any more: a type in value position meant "whichever instance
//     the solver picked", and there is no solver.
dump :: <M, indent: u32 = 2> -> fmt.Formattable<World<M>> {

    format = (v, w) -> () {
        io.write(w, "world {\n")
        for (i := 0; i < v.pos.count(); i += 1) {
            e := v.pos.entity_at(i)
            self.row(v, e, w)
        }
        io.write(w, "}\n")
    }

    // Creates a member; not one of Formattable's holes, so `::` and not `=`.
    // The glossary calls this out and it turned out to be the common case:
    // this instance has one hole and two helpers.
    row :: (v: World<M>, e: Entity, w: ptr<Writer>) -> () {
        self.pad(w)
        io.write(w, "#")
        fmt.fmt_u32.format(e.id, w)
        io.write(w, " ")
        match (v.pos.at(e)) { some (p) { vec2_fmt.format(p.*, w) }  none { } }
        match (v.hp.at(e))  { some (h) { io.write(w, " hp="); fmt.fmt_i32.format(h.hp, w) }  none { } }
        match (v.brain.at(e)) {
            some (a) {
                io.write(w, " ")
                io.write(w, match (a.*) {
                    idle     { "idle" }
                    hunt (_) { "hunt" }
                    flee (_) { "flee" }
                    dead     { "dead" }
                })
            }
            none { }
        }
        io.write(w, "\n")
    }

    pad :: (w: ptr<Writer>) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < indent; i += 1) { io.write(w, " ") }
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// main
// ============================================================================

main :: () -> () {
    heap  := mem.Heap()
    arena := mem.Arena(page, 1 << 20)

    // json.z's alias trick, applied to a second type. It works exactly as well
    // here, and it is worth noting that this line is the ONLY place in the
    // program below `World` that an allocator is named.
    A :: ptr<Arena>

    w := World<A>(&arena)
    w.wire()                                // <- FRICTION 21. Forget it and
    defer w.deinit()                        //    `despawn` silently does
                                            //    nothing at all.

    // THE OWNERSHIP DANCE. Every one of these is a local whose ADDRESS goes
    // into a list that outlives this statement, so declaration order here is
    // load-bearing and nothing in the language enforces it. Written in the
    // other order it compiles and dangles. That is what erasure-as-a-borrow
    // costs, and this is where the language says you pay it.
    move   := Movement<A>()
    decay  := Decay<A>(0.1)
    fight  := Combat<A>(3)
    doomed := List<Entity, A>(&arena)
    reap   := Reaper<A>(&doomed)

    sched := Schedule<A>(&arena)
    defer sched.deinit()
    sched.add(&move)                        // &Movement<A> -> ptr<System<A>>
    sched.add(&decay)
    sched.add(&fight)
    sched.add(&reap)

    // A stored closure. `obituary` is a block with one field (`&log`) and one
    // `()` member; `&obituary` is the coercion to `ptr<Fn<Entity, ()>>`, built
    // here and only here, exactly as `boxed : ptr<Allocator>` is in unified.z.
    log      := List<Entity, A>(&arena)
    obituary := on_death<A>(&log)
    w.died.on(&obituary)

    // Spawn: three predators and one very unlucky herbivore.
    for (i := 0; i < 3; i += 1) {
        e := w.spawn()
        // `f32_of` is invented. Neither file has a story for numeric
        // conversion, and it wants to be one more policy (`From<A, B>`).
        w.pos.add(e, Vec2(f32_of(i), 0.0))
        w.vel.add(e, Vec2(1.0, 0.0))
        w.hp.add(e, Health(10, 10))
        w.brain.add(e, Ai.idle())
    }

    prey := w.spawn()
    w.pos.add(prey, Vec2(0.5, 0.0))
    w.hp.add(prey, Health(4, 4))
    // No Velocity and no Ai: the join in `Movement` skips it, which is the
    // whole point of a component system and the one part of this program that
    // needed no comment while writing it.

    for (t := 0; t < 4; t += 1) { sched.tick(&w, 0.5) }

    io.write(io.out, fmt.to_string<F = dump<>>(heap, w))
    io.write(io.out, fmt.to_string<F = dump<indent = 4>>(heap, w))

    // Two calls, two formatters, both said out loud. The first line used to
    // read `fmt.to_string(heap, w)` and it was an ERROR: naming `dump` was
    // what took it out of the pool, so there was nothing left for that call to
    // find, and the fix was to publish the default a second time anonymously.
    // That was FRICTION 30. It is not a friction any more, because there is no
    // unnamed spelling left to be missing -- `dump<>` IS the default, written
    // where it is used.

    io.write(io.out, "died: ")
    io.write(io.out, fmt.to_string<F = fmt.fmt_u32>(heap, log.count))
}


// ============================================================================
// ============================================================================
//
//                                F I N D I N G S
//
// ============================================================================
// ============================================================================
//
// Numbering continues json.z: [W8+], FRICTION 13+, [P17+].
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT HELD UP
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// [W8]  A GENERIC TYPE ERASES TO A NON-GENERIC SHAPE when no hole in the shape
//       mentions the parameter. `Store<Vec2,M>` and `Store<Health,M>` are
//       different types with different vtables and both are `ptr<AnyStore>`.
//       This is NOTE 3's rule read backwards and it is what makes a
//       heterogeneous registry possible at all. Without it `despawn` would
//       have to name every component type in the application.
//
// [W9]  "PARAMS OF A `-> self` DEF ARE THE FIELDS" survives its second file
//       and is still the highest-value line in the language. A configured
//       system -- `Decay(0.1)`, `Combat(3)`, `Reaper(&doomed)` -- needs no
//       builder, no config struct and no new form, and a stateless one is
//       `Movement()`: no fields, zero bytes, one shared vtable behind
//       `ptr<System<M>>`.
//
// [W10] MATCH-AS-EXPRESSION IS WHAT MAKES SUM-TYPED STATE MACHINES READABLE.
//       The entire AI transition is one assignment:
//
//           a.* = match (a.*) { idle {...} hunt (t) {...} flee (to) {...} dead {...} }
//
//       Every arm has to produce the same type, so a forgotten transition is a
//       type error rather than a state that quietly never changes. This is the
//       strongest argument in either file for `match` being an expression.
//
// [W11] THE SHAPE/POLICY TEST ANSWERS QUESTIONS IT WAS NOT DESIGNED FOR --
//       AND CAN STILL BE OVERRULED. "Can there legitimately be two?" applied
//       to operators said `[]` and `()` are members and `+` is a policy. Half
//       of that stands and is the design: `[]` and `()` take `self`, there is
//       one way for a List to index itself, and both are members today for
//       exactly the reason the test gave.
//
//       The other half was right and lost. There ARE two ways to add two
//       Vec2 -- `saturating` is still on the page -- and `+` is not a policy
//       anyway, because a policy is reached by name and an operator has no
//       name in it. The test answers "is this one or many". It does not
//       answer "can the call site say WHICH", and that second question is the
//       one that decided operators. Keep it as a record of a good test's
//       limit: a one-line test that keeps producing the right answer is still
//       only answering its own question.
//
// [W12] [P16] PAYS OFF ON A PARAMETERISED INSTANCE. `dump<indent>` is one and
//       json.z never actually had one. `self.row(...)` carries `indent` down;
//       `dump.row(...)` resets every nested level to the default. Two
//       spellings, two distinct meanings, both visible in the text, and that
//       difference is the entire reason `self` exists inside an instance.
//
//       There was a third -- `Formattable<World<M>>.row(...)`, which did not
//       typecheck because `row` is a fresh member and not one of the type's
//       holes. That distinction was worth drawing while a type in value
//       position meant something. It does not any more, and losing the case
//       cost the win nothing: the two that remain are the two that mattered.
//
// [W13] THE ALIAS TRICK GENERALISES. `A :: ptr<Arena>` in main is the only
//       mention of an allocator in the whole application layer, exactly as
//       `Doc :: Json<ptr<Arena>>` was in json.z. Two unrelated programs, same
//       one-line fix, no library changes: this is now a pattern rather than a
//       coincidence and belongs in the language's documentation.
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHERE IT FOUGHT BACK
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Ordered by how much they cost, not by discovery.
//
// FRICTION 25 -- NOTHING SAYS WHO MAY WRITE WHAT, and in a simulation this
//   stops being a documentation problem. json.z raised it as FRICTION 8 with
//   `get` and `put` both taking `&self`. Here:
//
//     - `Store.at` hands out a `ptr<C>` into a `List` that reallocates on
//       `push`. The pointer is valid until anyone adds a component of that
//       type. Nothing in the signature, the shape or the call site says so.
//     - `Reaper.run` iterates `w.hp` and calls `w.died.emit`, which calls a
//       closure holding `ptr<World<M>>`, which may despawn -- which
//       swap-removes inside `w.hp`, under the loop. Three signatures, no
//       reentrancy anywhere in them.
//     - `Combat.chase` holds `me` and `prey`, two interior pointers into the
//       same store, and writes through one of them.
//
//   This is now the file's top item. `::`/`:=` encode immutable/mutable for
//   BINDINGS and there is still no counterpart for POINTEES, so a `ptr<T>` is
//   a fully mutable, unrestricted, indefinitely-lived handle. Note the shape
//   of the fix matters more than usual: the language's whole story is that
//   dispatch and cost are visible in the type, and aliasing is the one cost
//   that currently is not.
//
// FRICTION 21 -- A `-> self` BLOCK HAS NO CONSTRUCTOR BODY, so a structure
//   cannot refer to itself. See the long note above `World`. json.z met the
//   same wall (FRICTION 5) and read it as being about FAILURE; [P14]
//   (`-> Result<self, E>`) was adopted and gives the constructor a RETURN TYPE
//   while still giving it no BODY. `try` in a field initialiser has nothing to
//   unwind to and no place to run.
//
//   The cost here is two-phase construction -- `w.wire()` -- which is the
//   pattern the unified form was meant to abolish, and which is unchecked:
//   forget the line and `despawn` sweeps an empty list forever.  -> [P21]
//
// FRICTION 17 -- A CONTAINER STILL CANNOT BE FULLY ENCAPSULATED. `List` can
//   close its projection now that `[]` hands out a place, and no line in this
//   file reaches into a List field any more. `Map` cannot: json.z's own
//   printer reaches into `members.slots` and `members.cap` to ITERATE, and a
//   place does
//   not address iteration. json.z's [P5] says the projection is the export
//   list:
//
//       Map :: <K, V, M> (allocator: M) -> {get, put, remove, count, deinit} { ... }
//
//   Do that and the printer stops compiling. Half of what makes visibility
//   affordable on a container arrived with places; the other half is
//   iteration ([P23]), and until it does, a container that cannot be walked
//   through its own projection must export its storage.
//
// FRICTION 20 -- A SUM'S DISCRIMINANT IS NOT A VALUE. json.z's FRICTION 7
//   wanted `expect(Token.colon())` to mean "same variant, ignore payload".
//   Here the same gap blocks three separate things: a tag test (`is_dead`
//   spells out four arms to compute a bool), an archetype BITMASK (the actual
//   data structure a real ECS uses, which needs one bit per component type and
//   therefore an ordinal per variant), and serialisation (a saved game must
//   write a stable number for `Ai.hunt`). "Ignore the payload" was the small
//   version of this.  -> [P19]
//
// FRICTION 23 -- A PARTIAL PROJECTION IS NOT A TYPE. json.z's [W4] found
//   `-> {at, found}` to be the thing you reach for constantly. Its limit is
//   that `-> {a, b}` is a property of a DECLARATION and names nothing, so
//   `Option<{e: Entity, a: ptr<A>}>` cannot be written and every join result
//   has to become a named record (`Hit<A, B>`) -- which brings the plumbing
//   parameters with it. The multiple-return form does not compose with
//   generics, and that is precisely where container code needs it.
//
// FRICTION 24 -- ARITY FAMILIES REACH THE PUBLIC SURFACE. json.z called
//   `Fn`/`Fn2` "cheap enough to live with". `Join2`, `Join3`, `Join4` are not
//   an implementation detail; they are what a user of the ECS types every day,
//   and a system reading three components and writing a fourth is the ordinary
//   case, not the exotic one. Variadic compile-time args are the only real
//   answer and nothing cheaper was found.
//
// FRICTION 18 -- THERE IS NO `for (x in xs)`, so every traversal in this file
//   is a C index loop. `Iterator<T>` was the standard library's answer, both
//   files declared it, and it was used exactly once -- by `fold`, in json.z --
//   because a `loop` + `match` + `Option` consumer is four lines to replace
//   one and nobody writes those four lines. That was taken as the signal it
//   was: `Iterator`, `ListIter`, `iter` and `fold` are cut. The shape was
//   never the friction, though; the missing form still is.  -> [P23]
//
// FRICTION 28 -- POINTERS INTO A CONTAINER ARE THE ONLY MUTABLE HANDLE, and
//   the join hands out two per hit. `Combat.chase` then re-looks-up the same
//   entity in the same store because there is no way to keep the pointer the
//   join already found across a call that might have invalidated it. Half a
//   performance problem and half FRICTION 25.
//
// FRICTION 27 -- THE IDENTITY OF AN ERASED VALUE IS UNDEFINED, so `Signal` has
//   no `off`. A `ptr<Shape>` is two words built at the coercion site; whether
//   two coercions of one block compare equal is not stated, and the vtable
//   half is a compiler-chosen address. Any API that removes a registered
//   callback needs an answer -- probably a handle returned by `on`, but the
//   language-level question (is `ptr<Shape>` comparable) still has to be
//   settled.
//
// FRICTION 30 -- AN INSTANCE WAS EITHER POOLED OR NAMED, NEVER BOTH. Naming
//   was what removed an instance from the pool, so `dump` could not also be
//   the default `Formattable<World<M>>`, and `fmt.to_string(heap, w)` in main
//   had nothing to find. json.z hid it: `spaced` was a named SECOND instance
//   and an anonymous first one already existed. The idiom that resolved it was
//   a one-line forwarder republishing `dump<>` anonymously.
//
//   DISSOLVED, and it is the cleanest thing the cut bought. With no pool there
//   is no pooled/named distinction to fall on the wrong side of, no forwarder,
//   and no implicit default a reader has to already know. `dump<>` in
//   `to_string<F = dump<>>` says which formatter, in the call, every time. The
//   friction was never about `dump`. It was about a lookup that could silently
//   find nothing, and the answer turned out to be to stop looking things up.
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPOSALS
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// STATUS. [P19], [P21] and [P23] are open. [P22] is not: places were adopted,
// and it stays because the evidence for them is this file. [P17] is not
// either, for the opposite reason -- it was REJECTED, together with the pool
// it needed. It stays for the same reason: the evidence was this file, and a
// proposal that lost is worth more written down than deleted.
//
// [P17] REJECTED. ARITHMETIC AND COMPARISON OPERATORS ARE POLICIES; `[]` AND
//       `()` STAY MEMBERS. As proposed: `a + b` is `Add<A, B>.add(a, b)`,
//       `a == b` is `Eq<A, B>.eq(a, b)`, `a < b` is `Ord<A>.compare(a, b)`
//       tested against zero, every one of them found by the solver on the
//       operand types. `a[i]` and `a(x)` stay members, because they take
//       `self`.
//
//       Derived from rules.z's own test rather than chosen: two ways to add
//       two Vec2 (saturating, wrapping) -- yes, so a policy; two ways for a
//       List to index itself -- no, so a member. And everything awkward
//       dissolved at once:
//
//         - `2.0 * v` worked, because a policy is not attached to a type and
//           nobody has to own f32;
//         - primitives stopped being special: `Add<f32, f32>` was a pooled
//           instance like any other, and the language had no built-in
//           arithmetic, only instructions inside those instances;
//         - NOTE 4 was untouched, because these are instances, not extrinsics;
//         - `lerp :: <T> (a: T, b: T, t: f32) -> T` -- a generic over
//           "anything numeric" -- became sayable, which under the member
//           reading it is not, in any form;
//         - `Ord` and `Eq` were policies, so `<` and `==` landed where the
//           standard library already keeps them.
//
//       Every line of that is still true and none of it survived, because the
//       argument was never against a competing operator design. It was for
//       the POOL, and it did not know that was what it was arguing. A policy
//       is reached by NAME; `+` is the one call site in the language that
//       carries no name and cannot be given one; so operators-as-policies
//       needs an instance nobody named, which is the pool, which is the
//       solver. The pool went for reasons with nothing to do with arithmetic
//       -- an unnamed declaration is invisible, and what one resolves to is a
//       whole-program question -- and operator overloading went with it,
//       because it IS the solver rather than a customer of it.
//
//       `Ord`, `Formattable` and `Key` survive as policies precisely where
//       `Add` could not: `sort`, `to_string` and `Map` take them as ordinary
//       compile-time parameters, and an ordinary call site can name what it
//       passes. That is the whole difference, and it is a difference about
//       spelling, not about how many instances there legitimately are.
//
//       The cost is on the page above and it is real: `h.a.*.add(h.b.*.scale(dt))`
//       for an integration step, `t.eq(e)` for a handle comparison, no
//       `2.0 * v` in any form, and no `lerp` over `T` in any form. The
//       proposal's closing line was that it removed machinery rather than
//       adding it. That was true, and it is why it lost -- cutting the pool
//       removed more.
//
// [P21] THE BLOCK IS THE CONSTRUCTOR. A `-> self` block may contain plain
//       statements, run in textual order at construction, interleaved with the
//       `:=` field bindings. `self` becomes available at the point every `:=`
//       field is bound; before that, naming it is an error. With [P14]'s
//       `-> Result<self, E>` in the projection, `try` in that body means what
//       it means everywhere else.
//
//           World :: <M: Allocator> (allocator: M) -> Result<self, E> {
//               pos    := Store<Vec2, M>(allocator)
//               vel    := Store<Vec2, M>(allocator)
//               stores := try List<ptr<AnyStore>, M>(allocator)
//               stores.push(&self.pos)          // self is available: all
//               stores.push(&self.vel)          // fields are bound
//           }
//
//       This is not a new form -- a field initialiser is already a statement
//       that runs at construction; the proposal is that it need not be a
//       binding. It closes json.z's FRICTION 5, this file's FRICTION 21, and
//       makes [P14] mean something. What it needs underneath it -- that a
//       value has one address, so `&self.pos` survives the return -- it has.
//
// [P22] PLACES ARE A PROJECTION -- the third form, which neither option
//       below anticipated. `-> &T` says a LOCATION escapes, alongside `-> T`,
//       `-> self`, `-> {a, b}` and `-> oneof`.
//
//         [] :: (&self, i: u32) -> &T { self.items[i] }
//
//       Two spellings were considered here and both were wrong:
//
//         (a) `[] :: (&self, i: u32) -> ptr<T>` with an explicit deref at the
//             use site, `xs[i].* = v`. Costs no syntax, and makes `xs[i]` --
//             the shortest spelling -- mean the
//             ADDRESS of the element, when every reader alive reads it as the
//             element. `v := xs[i]` would compile and hand back a pointer.
//         (b) a second member `[]=`, which is C++'s split and reintroduces the
//             paired-name problem [P15] removed.
//
//       The projection form keeps `xs[i]` meaning the element and `&xs[i]`
//       meaning its address, as in every language a reader has already used,
//       and it costs no new pointer type: `-> &T` is `ptr<T>` underneath and
//       appears only in projection position. It is what makes [P5]'s
//       visibility usable on a container at all (FRICTION 17), and `List` in
//       unified.z now exports six names and hides its storage.
//
// [P23] `for (x := it) { ... }` over anything with a `next` that returns
//       `Option<T>`, desugaring to the `loop` + `match` + `Option` consumer
//       written by hand four times in this file. `for (h := join2(&w.pos,
//       &w.vel))` is then the whole of a system's traversal. `Iterator<T>`
//       was the standard library's attempt at this without the form, and it
//       was cut: both files declared it, neither used it, because the manual
//       consumer is four times the size of the index loop it replaces. That
//       was the argument for the form, not against it -- the shape carried
//       nothing the desugaring does not already need structurally.
//
// [P19] A SUM'S DISCRIMINANT IS A VALUE. For `S :: -> oneof {...}`, `S.tag` is
//       a derived sum of unit variants, `tag_of(v): S.tag` is total, and it is
//       comparable, ordinal, and usable as an array index or a bit position.
//       `expect(Token.colon())` becomes `tag_of(t) == Token.tag.colon`, an
//       archetype mask becomes `1 << ordinal_of(tag)`, and a save file has a
//       stable number to write. Closes json.z's FRICTION 7 and this file's
//       FRICTION 20.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS FILE SAYS ABOUT json.z's OPEN ITEMS
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   CONFIRMED, WORSE THAN STATED
//     FRICTION 8 (mutability) is not a documentation gap, it is the file's
//     top item -- see FRICTION 25. Interior pointers are the only mutable
//     handle, and they are invalidated by ordinary container operations.
//
//     FRICTION 7 (tag comparison) blocks a data structure, not a convenience:
//     no ordinal, no bitmask, no archetypes, no serialisation.
//
//     FRICTION 6 (arity families) becomes public API at a query interface.
//
//   CONFIRMED, AS STATED
//     FRICTION 2 (monomorphisation is viral) reappears in a SHAPE --
//     `System<M>` -- where the alias trick cannot reach, because a library
//     cannot name the application's allocator. The alias still fixes the
//     application layer, in one line, in a second unrelated program ([W13]).
//
//   DOWNGRADED
//     FRICTION 3 (no refutable match) barely showed up. The one-arm-plus-`_`
//     binding idiom absorbed almost every case here, including four in
//     `Combat.chase`. What is left is `is_dead`, which [P19] handles.
//
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE-PARAGRAPH VERDICT
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// json.z built a tree and printed it, and found the language's missing
// vocabulary -- sums, `try`, `match`, `self`. This file MUTATES and STORES,
// and everything it found is downstream of those two verbs. The declaration
// form itself did not bend: `-> self` blocks as systems, generic stores erased
// to one shape, a policy as a state machine's formatter, all of it worked.
//
// The one thing this file argued for and lost is operators-as-policies
// ([P17]), and it lost to a property of the POOL rather than to a better
// operator design -- which is the most useful way to lose, because what
// replaced it is shorter to specify and every dispatch above is now written
// where it happens. The bill is `h.a.*.add(h.b.*.scale(dt))`, and it is
// itemised rather than hidden, which is the trade the whole language makes.
//
// What is missing is not more declaration syntax. The language can say what a
// value IS and where it lives; what it still cannot say is WHO MAY WRITE IT
// and for how long. Places, one address per value, and no destructor answer
// most of the memory question, and this file is where each of them is paid
// for by hand -- in `wire`, in `deinit`, and in main's declaration order.
// What is left of that question is the constructor body ([P21]) and aliasing,
// and mutability (FRICTION 25) is what the second one costs today.
// ============================================================================
